.TH KTON 1 "2026-07-07" "kton v0" "kton manual"
.SH NAME
kton \- the cockpit that conducts plankton + nekton (network, anchoring, pinning)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kton
.I command
[\fIarguments\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kton
orchestrates
.BR plankton (1)
and
.BR nekton (1)
but \fBreimplements nothing\fR - every operation is a call into a kernel. Delete kton and each
operation is still runnable directly.
.PP
It exists to hold the surface deliberately kept OUT of the kernels so they stay minimal and
WebAssembly-compilable: the \fBnetwork\fR (serving a port, mirroring a URL peer), the
\fBtransparency log\fR (Rekor anchoring), and optional \fBbyte pinning\fR. Pure, local
overlay-by-hash federation stays in the kernels (\fBplankton mirror\fR / \fBnekton mirror\fR on a
directory); kton adds the network peer. Like the kernels, kton never executes your programs.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B kton mirror plankton \fIpeer\fR [--pin]
Pull + persist a peer plankton registry. \fIpeer\fR is a URL served by any implementation of the
SPEC Clause 12 queries, or a local registry directory (read directly, no port). \fB--pin\fR (URL
peer only) also fetches and verifies the bytes. kton ships the federation CLIENT and no server: the
queries are normative, the transport is not (SPEC Annex C), and a listening socket carries
authentication and rate-limiting obligations that belong to a deployment. \fBplankton records
--json --since\fR \fIN\fR answers the same sync query on stdout.
.TP
.B kton mirror nekton \fIpeer\fR [--with-material]
Pull + persist a peer nekton registry (URL or local directory). \fB--with-material\fR additionally
makes this copy of the SPEC 8.1 verification material complete, asking the peer about every claim
held rather than about the last batch - material is attached out of band and after the fact, so a
batch would miss it.
.TP
.B kton anchor \fIenvelope.dsse.json\fR \fIpubkey.pub\fR|\fIhex\fR [--store]
Anchor a signed record in the Rekor transparency log (Sigstore) and verify the inclusion proof at
anchor time. \fB--store\fR records the verified entry as SPEC 8.1 verification material on the
record, which is what the long-term-verifiability clause asks for; without it the proof only reaches
stdout, and a year from now verification depends on the service still answering. There is still no
offline re-verification of a saved proof. The public key may be a .pub file path or the hex string.
.TP
.B kton pin \fIfile\fR
Pin a file's bytes into the plankton blob store (optional; the kernel stores no bytes).
.TP
.B kton blob \fIsha256:...\fR
Is this content pinned locally?
.TP
.B kton fetch \fIsha256:...\fR --trust-keys \fIdir\fR [--allow-local]
Resolve content to its bytes via nekton \fBlocated-at\fR claims (predicate \fBdcat:downloadURL\fR)
\fBsigned by a key you named\fR: dereference each verified URI, verify the SHA-256, and pin.
.B --trust-keys
is REQUIRED, and it is a directory of .pub keys. Ingest does not verify signatures (the wire carries
a keyid, not a key), so "the claim is in the registry" says nothing about who put it there -
without a trust policy the locators are listed and nothing is opened. Dereferencing is a request
made from this host, and for \fBfile://\fR a read of this disk, which the hash check afterwards
cannot undo.
.B --allow-local
additionally permits \fBfile://\fR and addresses on this host or network; both are refused by
default, redirects are re-checked against the same rule, and the body is bounded. The kernels never
resolve URIs - this is a cockpit job.
.TP
.B kton man
Print this manual page (roff source; pipe to \fBman -l -\fR to render). Embedded in the binary.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PLANKTON_DIR
plankton registry directory (default ./plankton-data).
.TP
.B NEKTON_DIR
nekton registry directory (default ./nekton-data).
.SH EXAMPLES
Share one session's results with another, with no network at all:
.RS
.nf

  PLANKTON_DIR=B plankton mirror ../A/plankton-data
.fi
.RE
.PP
Over a network, against any peer that answers the SPEC Clause 12 queries:
.RS
.nf

  PLANKTON_DIR=B kton mirror plankton https://peer.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read a registry's records - envelopes included - as the same document a peer would be served:
.RS
.nf

  plankton records --json --since 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fetch content, naming whose locations you accept:
.RS
.nf

  kton fetch sha256:... --trust-keys ./trusted-keys
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR plankton (1),
.BR nekton (1)
